This file should display the chat interface. Taking the name_call variable from the saved ini file generated by name_call.py file. This file's purpose is to listen to radio responses provided by yac.py and present them as a chat terminal interface. So that the braille display driver being used can show the radio's output in braille.
Use the compact interface accepted after the lite experiment. Show neither yac: nor the user's name during radio operation. If the saved name is missing or invalid, present the name_call module during setup.
Example:
S 7 7.159 LSB

Do not echo command keys. Display processed radio responses on separate lines, keeping the cursor at the end of each response until the next action. Keep editable numeric values visible, as in the accepted test, and use e: for frequency entry. Keep the localized step-size question to identify the value being edited.
Use the saved name in the opening welcome and closing goodbye only. When the user presses q, display the localized goodbye followed by the saved name (for example Bye Gena), and close the radio cleanly.
Notice that the chat interface only requires a press of the enter key to indicate the user has finished entering a frequency. There may be other occasions when the user is required to press the enter key. But pressing enter should be avoided unless necessary.
